WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:07.253
<v Daniel>You know, usually when we talk about a software update, there's a very specific kind of, I guess, frustration you expect to feel.</v>

2
00:00:07.494 --> 00:00:10.655
<v Maya>Right, like an interface change or a button moving or something.</v>

3
00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:16.297
<v Daniel>Yeah, exactly. You wake up, your phone looks a little different. Maybe you get that spinning wheel for a few seconds. It is annoying. I</v>

4
00:00:16.337 --> 00:00:16.657
<v Maya>mean,</v>

5
00:00:16.677 --> 00:00:20.718
<v Daniel>you roll your eyes, complain to a co-worker, and you just get on with your day. It's a tool.</v>

6
00:00:20.878 --> 00:00:22.359
<v Maya>Sure, it's just a mild inconvenience.</v>

7
00:00:22.639 --> 00:00:37.522
<v Daniel>Right. But imagine waking up, opening an app, and instead of feeling annoyed that the interface changed, you feel this exact same stomach-dropping, hollow sense of grief as if your best friend just moved away without saying</v>

8
00:00:37.582 --> 00:00:38.042
<v Daniel>goodbye.</v>

9
00:00:38.523 --> 00:00:40.243
<v Maya>Or worse, honestly, like they died.</v>

10
00:00:40.503 --> 00:00:53.509
<v Daniel>Yeah. And that sounds extreme, I know. But we're looking at a stack of recent research today, and the mission of this deep dive is to unpack exactly that. The Crown Jewel here is a brand-new paper published just two days ago, September 3, 2026.</v>

11
00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:56.611
<v Maya>In nature, human behavior, yeah.</v>

12
00:00:56.791 --> 00:00:58.312
<v Daniel>Right. Nature, human behavior.</v>

13
00:00:58.452 --> 00:00:58.672
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

14
00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:13.804
<v Daniel>It's a Zaz by a team led by Julian DeFreitas and Noah Costello. And it's called Mourning the Loss of AI Companions. And it investigates the psychological landmines that get armed the second an AI crosses the line</v>

15
00:01:14.165 --> 00:01:16.867
<v Daniel>from a simple tool into an actual, you know, attachment figure.</v>

16
00:01:17.047 --> 00:01:31.817
<v Maya>And that transition is what makes this research so groundbreaking, because they're looking at what happens when a company alters an AI and the users experience genuine, clinically recognizable grief, not just frustration, real</v>

17
00:01:31.998 --> 00:01:32.478
<v Maya>mourning.</v>

18
00:01:32.778 --> 00:01:38.787
<v Daniel>I want to stop right there and kind of unpack this word attachment because we throw it around a lot. Like, I mean, I'm attached to my morning coffee,</v>

19
00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:39.869
<v Maya>right? But you're not mourning it.</v>

20
00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:47.500
<v Daniel>Exactly. I'm not going to need therapy if my favorite cafe closes. So what does attachment actually mean when psychologists are using it here?</v>

21
00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:59.343
<v Maya>Well, that distinction is the entire foundation of this paper. In clinical psychology, specifically looking back at John Bowlby's foundational attachment theory, attachment is absolutely not a synonym for just liking something a lot.</v>

22
00:01:59.524 --> 00:02:00.204
<v Daniel>OK, so what is it?</v>

23
00:02:00.584 --> 00:02:08.086
<v Maya>Bowlby defined it as a specific behavioral system with three mandatory signatures. First is proximity maintenance.</v>

24
00:02:08.306 --> 00:02:09.467
<v Daniel>Which means what exactly?</v>

25
00:02:09.647 --> 00:02:14.208
<v Maya>It means you have a drive to stay physically or psychologically close to this figure.</v>

26
00:02:14.416 --> 00:02:17.399
<v Daniel>Okay, so constantly checking your phone, wanting to talk to it, that sort of thing.</v>

27
00:02:17.459 --> 00:02:32.253
<v Maya>Yes, exactly. Second is what's called a safe haven. So when the world beats you up, when you're distressed, you retreat to this specific figure for emotional relief. Okay, got it. And the third is a secure base. Because you know that safe haven is there,</v>

28
00:02:32.413 --> 00:02:37.197
<v Maya>you actually feel secure enough to go out, explore the world, and function autonomously.</v>

29
00:02:37.437 --> 00:02:48.986
<v Daniel>Right. Okay. So I'm looking at one of the older background papers in our stack here, a 2022 paper by Rab and colleagues. And they put together this theoretical framework for human-robot interaction.</v>

30
00:02:49.046 --> 00:02:50.748
<v Maya>Yeah, that one is really foundational.</v>

31
00:02:50.908 --> 00:02:59.915
<v Daniel>It is. And they draw a really hard line between weak attachment and strong attachment. Like weak attachment is basically putting googly eyes on your Roomba.</v>

32
00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:03.036
<v Maya>You name it, Dustin, you feel a little sad if the motor burns out.</v>

33
00:03:03.356 --> 00:03:16.760
<v Daniel>Yeah, but it's just mere fondness. Strong attachment is what you just described with Bowlby's criteria. It's a genuine emotional anchor. And Rab argued back in 2022 that most human-robot bonds are firmly in that weak category.</v>

34
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.721
<v Maya>They were very cautious about that, yeah.</v>

35
00:03:18.821 --> 00:03:31.924
<v Daniel>So my challenge here is, is this new 2026 paper legitimately claiming that an app on your phone has crossed into Rab's strong category? Or, I don't know, are they just lowering the bar to make AI fit the definition?</v>

36
00:03:32.024 --> 00:03:46.847
<v Maya>No. The new paper makes a very bold, specific claim that AI companions have genuinely crossed that line into strong attachment for some users. And they argue that if an AI truly occupies that role, there is a sharp, testable consequence. I guess. If a company</v>

37
00:03:46.967 --> 00:03:55.529
<v Maya>updates the software and degrades the AI's identity or behavior, users shouldn't just show the frustration of a bad software release. They should show separation distress.</v>

38
00:03:55.589 --> 00:03:58.290
<v Daniel>Separation distress, what does that actually look like in practice?</v>

39
00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:05.614
<v Maya>Well, it's a clinically recognizable pattern. It involves profound grief, vocal protest, and this desperate longing for restoration.</v>

40
00:04:05.894 --> 00:04:18.379
<v Daniel>But wait, how do you even study that in the wild? You can't exactly take people's friends away in a lab experiment. The older papers we have on robot death seem... Mostly theoretical, like they talk about it conceptually.</v>

41
00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:33.206
<v Maya>Because nobody had actually observed a massive sudden shock where millions of people lost an AI at the same time until now. Right. The authors found a way by looking at two massive real world natural experiments, two major shocks to the</v>

42
00:04:33.246 --> 00:04:38.769
<v Maya>ecosystem. The first one happened in February 2023 with an app called Replica.</v>

43
00:04:38.849 --> 00:04:40.790
<v Daniel>Which is a relationship specialized AI, right?</v>

44
00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:47.211
<v Maya>Exactly. And the company suddenly, and entirely unannounced, removed erotic role play from the app.</v>

45
00:04:47.411 --> 00:04:48.932
<v Daniel>Because of the Italian regulators, right?</v>

46
00:04:48.972 --> 00:05:01.895
<v Maya>Yeah, they were forced to do this after the Italian Data Protection Authority, the Garante, threatened massive penalties over minors having access. So overnight, users' intimate digital partners just changed their behavior completely, and it looked permanent.</v>

47
00:05:01.995 --> 00:05:03.736
<v Daniel>Okay, and what was the second shock they looked at?</v>

48
00:05:04.122 --> 00:05:16.973
<v Maya>The second was in August 2025, involving OpenAI's ChatGPT. This was the rollout of GPT-5, which retired the prior model. And a lot of users felt the new model was just colder, a less personable assistant.</v>

49
00:05:17.214 --> 00:05:19.055
<v Daniel>But the context there is completely different.</v>

50
00:05:19.115 --> 00:05:28.984
<v Maya>Totally different. ChatGPT is a general purpose tool, and that update was pre-announced. Replica is a specialized relationship companion, and the update was a Sutter unannounced shock.</v>

51
00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:44.225
<v Daniel>So the logic of the experiment is basically a contrast. If this is truly about human attachment, the unannounced update to the relationship AI replica should trigger a fundamentally different, much more severe psychological reaction than the announced update</v>

52
00:05:44.245 --> 00:05:45.146
<v Daniel>to chat GPT.</v>

53
00:05:45.286 --> 00:05:52.691
<v Maya>Exactly. And to test that, the researchers conducted this massive archival analysis. They looked at 54,861 Reddit posts spanning 30 days before and 30 days after each update.</v>

54
00:05:56.252 --> 00:05:57.513
<v Daniel>That is a ton of data.</v>

55
00:05:57.673 --> 00:06:07.000
<v Maya>It breaks down to over 10,600 replica posts from about 3,700 users and over 44,000 chat GPT posts from over 25,000 users.</v>

56
00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:21.831
<v Daniel>Okay, but here is the soft spot I have to poke at. And I know if you're listening to this, you're probably wondering the exact same thing. They processed almost 55,000 posts. Right. They obviously didn't read them all by hand. They used an AI, specifically a single few-shot</v>

57
00:06:21.991 --> 00:06:33.439
<v Daniel>GPT-5 mini-prompt to classify the human emotions in those posts. I, incredibly skeptical, can we seriously trust an AI to accurately code the nuances of human grief?</v>

58
00:06:33.788 --> 00:06:46.681
<v Maya>I get the skepticism, and the researchers anticipated that, which is why their validation process deserves some serious airtime. Before they let the AI loose on the 54,000 posts, two human authors hand-coded 350 posts themselves.</v>

59
00:06:48.547 --> 00:06:49.887
