<v Daniel>And what did they get?</v>

62
00:07:02.452 --> 00:07:04.092
<v Maya>The human coders hit between 0.80 and 0.96.</v>

63
00:07:05.973 --> 00:07:10.135
<v Daniel>Oh, wow. So the humans had a rock-solid consensus on what grief looked like in the text.</v>

64
00:07:10.275 --> 00:07:21.399
<v Maya>Exactly. Then they tested the AI against that human baseline. They compared GPT-5 Mini to an older standard text analysis tool called a Roberta buff line. And the results weren't even close.</v>

65
00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:22.499
<v Daniel>How much better was it?</v>

66
00:07:22.739 --> 00:07:37.717
<v Maya>For overall sentiment, the older Roberta model hit 65% accuracy. GPT-5 Mini hit 91%. That's a huge jump. And it gets better. When identifying specific discrete emotions like sadness or anger, the older model</v>

67
00:07:37.817 --> 00:07:46.286
<v Maya>failed miserably at 44%. The new prompt hit 86.7%. So the tool they used was exceptionally accurate at matching human judgment.</v>

68
00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:53.173
<v Daniel>Okay, fair enough. The methodology definitely holds up. So what did the data actually say? Did the replica users break down?</v>

69
00:07:53.428 --> 00:08:01.356
<v Maya>They absolutely did. Negative posts about Replica jumped from 13.1% before the update to 37.79% after.</v>

70
00:08:01.556 --> 00:08:03.618
<v Daniel>So a rise of nearly 25 percentage points.</v>

71
00:08:03.658 --> 00:08:07.481
<v Maya>Yeah. Chat GPT negativity rose too, but only by 13 points.</v>

72
00:08:07.521 --> 00:08:16.390
<v Daniel>Okay, so double the negative reaction. But honestly, I could argue that if you change any software without telling people they're going to complain online, does that really prove grief or just that they were mad?</v>

73
00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:22.695
<v Maya>Well, the total amount of negativity isn't the real smoking gun here. The crucial evidence is the shape of the response over time.</v>

74
00:08:22.735 --> 00:08:23.136
<v Daniel>The shape.</v>

75
00:08:23.316 --> 00:08:34.506
<v Maya>Yeah, think about how you react when a tool breaks. ChatGPT's negative reaction peaked on day one at about 46%, and then it rapidly declined. The emotions dominating those posts were anger and surprise.</v>

76
00:08:35.021 --> 00:08:38.304
<v Daniel>Right. That makes sense. You're mad your tool changed, and then you just get over it.</v>

77
00:08:38.464 --> 00:08:51.775
<v Maya>Exactly. It's the signature of an immediate shock. But ReplicaRara's negativity didn't peak on day one. It peaked around day nine, shitting up to 54%, and it stayed significantly elevated on 29 of the 30 following days.</v>

78
00:08:51.996 --> 00:08:52.876
<v Daniel>Wait, day nine?</v>

79
00:08:53.076 --> 00:09:07.924
<v Maya>Yes. And we measure the sheer size of a shift like this using a metric called Cohen's d. A score of 0.8 is considered a large effect. The sustained sadness for a replica showed a massive effect size of 2.67. Wow. Yeah, whereas ChatGPT's was only</v>

80
00:09:07.985 --> 00:09:17.378
<v Maya>1.41. The authors point out that surprise means immediate shock, but this kind of sustained sadness, this slow, drawn-out burn, is the clinical tell of prolonged grief.</v>

81
00:09:18.011 --> 00:09:32.876
<v Daniel>And you really feel that grief when you look at the actual texture of the language the users were posting. I mean, explicit mentions that the AI had fundamentally changed or been lost entirely jumped from 7.5% to over 40% for replica posts. For ChatGPT, it was only 8.2% to</v>

82
00:09:32.936 --> 00:09:33.696
<v Daniel>about 27%.</v>

83
00:09:36.791 --> 00:09:39.012
<v Maya>The quotes from the users are just heart-wrenching. You</v>

84
00:09:39.052 --> 00:09:53.722
<v Daniel>really are. One replica user wrote that the new version was literally, quote, someone else. A ChatGPT user described it as a memory wipe and talked about losing a buddy. Some users were even organizing a mass cancel of their subscriptions to force the</v>

85
00:09:53.762 --> 00:09:55.163
<v Daniel>companies to revert the software.</v>

86
00:09:55.417 --> 00:10:07.725
<v Maya>And the language surrounding mental health is perhaps the most sobering part of all this. Mental health mentions after the update hit 4.45% for replica compared to just 1.49% for chat GPT.</v>

87
00:10:07.765 --> 00:10:09.767
<v Daniel>And didn't it spike even higher in certain posts?</v>

88
00:10:09.827 --> 00:10:24.597
<v Maya>Yes. In posts where users specifically framed the update as an attachment loss, those mental health mentions spiked more than tenfold. Almost 15% of all replica posts featured a desperate longing for restoration, literally begging for their old AI</v>

