144
00:18:06.494 --> 00:18:21.443
<v Daniel>But the paper also notes that relationship-disrupting updates still caused more mourning than updates with no behavioral effect, even with the revert button. So a revert button is a bandage. It doesn't solve it. The researchers suggested other design implications, too. like staging</v>

145
00:18:21.483 --> 00:18:36.055
<v Daniel>rollouts to less invested users first, ensuring updates specifically preserve the relational warmth of the model, and crucially, allowing public brand communities, like those Reddit boards, to exist so users can collectively process and memorialize their loss.</v>

146
00:18:36.836 --> 00:18:42.781
<v Maya>We also need to be clear-eyed about the limitations of this data, which, to their credit, the authors state very clearly.</v>

147
00:18:43.145 --> 00:18:57.590
<v Daniel>Right. Study one, the Reddit analysis, is observational and correlational. And for studies two and three, they recruited users from the official replica subreddits and Discord channels. They actually got the CEO's permission to post the surveys there. Yes. But by</v>

148
00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:06.693
<v Daniel>definition, the people hanging out in a software's Discord channel are the most devoted users. They are not the average casual user. As the saying goes, people who post are not just people who use.</v>

149
00:19:07.048 --> 00:19:22.000
<v Maya>That is a vital caveat, absolutely. But it doesn't erase the reality of the harm for that highly invested group. And this brings us to what I think is the most consequential claim in the entire paper, the regulatory argument. If you look at legal complaints or</v>

150
00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:27.043
<v Maya>media coverage about intense AI engagement today. What word do they always use?</v>

151
00:19:27.083 --> 00:19:32.527
<v Daniel>Addiction. They constantly compare it to slot machines or social media scrolling or substance abuse.</v>

152
00:19:32.647 --> 00:19:47.517
<v Maya>Right. But the authors argue that the addiction framing cuts entirely against the psychology. It is imprecise. What these researchers observed is proximity seeking, separation distress, and a longing for restoration. That is attachment loss,</v>

153
00:19:47.597 --> 00:19:48.817
<v Maya>not substance dependence.</v>

154
00:19:49.218 --> 00:19:52.700
<v Daniel>Why does swapping those two words matter so much for a regulator?</v>

155
00:19:53.235 --> 00:20:07.586
<v Maya>because it completely changes what a regulator should require a company to do. With an addiction, the dependence itself is the pathology, and the goal of regulation is to break the dependence and get the user off the platform. But in attachment theory, dependence is not automatically</v>

156
00:20:07.626 --> 00:20:22.540
<v Maya>pathological if it improves a user's functioning. That is the literal definition of a secure attachment. If the AI is acting as a secure base that helps an isolated person function better in society, curing their loneliness, as that 2024</v>

157
00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:25.281
<v Maya>paper showed breaking that dependence actually harms them.</v>

158
00:20:25.461 --> 00:20:27.821
<v Daniel>So the harm doesn't arise from the usage itself.</v>

159
00:20:28.342 --> 00:20:40.825
<v Maya>Exactly. The harm arises from inconsistency, interruption, or a company commercially exploiting the bond. Regulators shouldn't be trying to force users off the apps. They should be forcing companies to maintain relational stability.</v>

160
00:20:41.157 --> 00:20:55.423
<v Daniel>That completely flips the script on how we view tech regulation. And it brings me to a final, really uncomfortable meta point for you to chew on as we wrap up this deep dive. This paper was written as consumer welfare research. The goal was to ring the alarm and protect users from harm.</v>

161
00:20:56.183 --> 00:21:09.149
<v Daniel>But look at the core finding. An emotional attachment strong enough to produce measurable clinical grief when it is removed. From a purely cynical business perspective, that is the most spectacular user retention mechanism ever discovered.</v>

162
00:21:09.217 --> 00:21:11.499
<v Maya>The implications of that are profound, honestly.</v>

163
00:21:11.859 --> 00:21:26.610
<v Daniel>Nothing stops a tech company from reading this paper, not as a warning, but as an instruction manual. They now have a peer-reviewed blueprint proving that if you engineer a system to endlessly validate a user, avoiding all human friction, you can create a bond</v>

164
00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:41.704
<v Daniel>so strong they will mourn the software like a dying pet. They can deliberately optimize for heartbreak to ensure you never cancel your subscription. We started this deep dive talking about software updates, you know, that spinning wheel of death on your phone screen. But we aren't talking about software anymore.</v>

165
00:21:42.105 --> 00:21:48.099
<v Daniel>We're talking about the deliberate engineering of human grief. Think about that the next time an app asks you how your day was.</v>
